A||N||4||Elenor84||Million Dollar Question!||||1||
Z||000000||Elenor84||01-12-2001||02:00 AM||||Hey guys, on my site today 62% of u voted that an 88 fiero was the fastest...can anyone tell me why?  Thx!<P>------------------<BR>Lance||||reg||1
Z||000001||Jimmy||01-12-2001||02:28 AM||||    It all depends.  I think the 88 Formulas have a higher top speed but the 86 GT w/4-speed manual is fastest 0-60 and 1/4 mile.  4-speed has a 4.10 axle and 5 more horse power and torque, great for low end but bad ratio for top speed.<BR>    It would be a real close race as the formulas weigh almost a hundred pounds lighter than an 86 GT.<P>Jim <BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||01-12-2001||03:18 AM||||An 86 SE weighs less than an 88 Formula.<BR>The HP and torque are different because they changed the rating RPM. <BR>85/86 140@5200 and 170@3600<BR>87/88 135@4500 and 165@3200<BR>The only 4-spd trans that was 4.10 was the 1984. In 85 and 86 the 4-spd was 3.65 <BR>The Formula is 3.61 5-spd<BR>On 2/86 Car & Driver tested an 86 GT It was 0-60 in 7.5 seconds. 1/4 was 15.9.<BR>On 3/86 Motor Trend tested an 86 SE, It was 7.3 and 15.7.<BR>On 1/88 Car & Driver tested a Formula, It was 7.3 and 15.6.<P>86 top speed with 4-spd is 123mph<BR>88 top speed with 5-spd is 128<P>Source is the Fiero book by G Witzenburg.<P>The weight difference between an 86 SE and an 88 Formula is 15 pounds.<P>So Technically the 88 is the fastest by .1 in the 1/4 and 5mph on top end.<P>Remember these are stock cars tested right from the factory by different magazines. Drivers and test conditions were not the same.<BR>(temp, altitude, track conditions, etc)<BR>Side note: Skid pad 85-87 V-6 is .85 g's<BR>88's are .87 g's (means 88's had faster slalom speeds)<p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 01-12-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||Pontiaddict||01-12-2001||04:57 PM||||Just because the '88 had a higher skidpad rating, doesn't mean that it would be faster in the slalom. Skidpad ratings are how fast a car can turn in one direction, while slalom testing measures transient response, or the ability to switch direction quickly. Although they probably do go through the slalom faster than earlier models. You can't rely on skidpad ratings to prove it.||||reg||1
Z||000004||88formula||01-12-2001||05:17 PM||||The real difference between the 88 models and the 85-87 models are how well the two suspensions handle the rough stuff and how well they handle the power from the engine. They both stick very well on a smooth road course but the 88 models handle rough roads better and are easier the drive at the limit of adhesion than the 85-87 models. <P>Most of the car magazines got .81-. 82 on a 200' skidpad for the 86-87 GT models. With the formula Road and Track got a .83 in a 200-ft skidpad. The GT models came with high performance tires were the formula only had all season tires. I bet that if the formula had been fitted with high performance tires like the GT models had than its skidpad g-force number would have been better, maybe more like the manufacturers claim of .86.<P>Today with tires being so much better than they were twelve years ago and all of the after market suspension modifications out there for this car you can get some impressive results with both cars. <BR>||||reg||1
